\cf0 Step 3) Combine the normalized counts and the top 10 up- and down-regulated genes.\
From both files, it will have the stats, log change, the P value, and the count values for each sample/gene.\
Tool: Join two Datasets (side by side on a specified field)\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.1.3\
Parameters: Join the top 10 file using column 1 with the normalized counts file and column 1; Set \'93Keep the header lines\'94 to true.\
\
Step 4) Remove unnecessary columns.\
Original dataset has extraneous information and only wanted to keep columns 8-14.\
Tool: Cut (columns from a table)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.0.2\
Parameters: Cut columns \'93c8-c14\'94; From \'93Join two datasets\'94\
Notes: Only wanted to have all gene names in column 1, plate replicates, and broth replicates.\
\
Step 5) Create a heat map.\
Used to visualize portions of the data. Typically, the darker (or more intense) the color is, the greater the expression level.\
Tool: Heatmap2\
Version: Galaxy Version 3.3.0+galaxy0\
Parameters: Input should be the result from the cutting tool; data transformation: Log2(value+1) transform my data; colormap pattern can vary (mine was the blues)\
\
Step 6) Filter for potential coding sequences.\
Want to add a column that will tell us what the genes are doing. Need to remove noncoding sequences from the annotation (gff) file.\
Tool: Filter (data on any column using simple expressions)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.1.1\
Parameters: Filter the promicromonospora.gff file; set with the following condition to \'93c3=\'91CDS\'92\'94; all else default.\
Notes: Used for associating gene functions.\
\
Step 7) Keep only column 9 (attributes).\
Output file from step 6 has extraneous information and only want to keep column 9 (attributes)\
Tool: Cut (columns from a table)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.0.2\
Parameters: Set cut columns to c9 using the output of the prior step.\
\
Step 8) Separate all the information into multiple columns.\
Information is grouped into one column and are separated by semicolon. Want to separate the information into multiple columns for ease of use.\
Tool: Convert (delimiters to TAB)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.0.1\
Parameters: Set convert all (delimiters) to \'93semicolons\'94; the dataset should be set to the output of step 7; all else default\
\
Step 9) Replace extraneous text.\
Column two lists the gene name but also has \'93Parent=\'93 which is extra information. Want to remove this part of the gene name.\
Tool: Replace Text (in a specific column)\
Version: Galaxy Version 9.5+galaxy3\
Parameters: Use the output of the previous step; in column should be set to \'93Column:2\'94; set the find pattern option to \'93Parent=\'94\
\
Step 10) Cut out other extraneous information.\
Only want to keep the gene name (column 2) and the gene product (column 8).\
Tool: Cut (columns from a table)\
Version: Galaxy Version 1.0.2\
Parameters: Set cut columns to \'93c2,c8\'94; apply this on the output from the prior step; all else default.\
\
Step 11) Combine the gene name, product, and expression together.\
Want all information side-by-side and in one file. \
Tool: Join two datasets (side by side on a specified field)\
Version: Galaxy Version 2.1.3\
Parameters: Set join to the output of the prior step and set using column to \'93Column: 1\'94; set with to the Top10List and set and column to \'93Column: 1\'94; set keep the header lines to \'93Yes\'94; all else default.}